    "title": "Lake Highland Prep's shooting stars deny Bolles girls basketball - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Clayton Freeman ,\n2 days ago Bolles' dreams of a hometown basketball championship finished beneath a 3-point barrage at UNF Arena.\nEight-time champion Orlando Lake Highland Prep sank threes early and often to end the Bulldogs' run 64-52 in the Florida High School Athletic Association Class 3A girls basketball semifinals at the University of North Florida on March 9.\nWhen Bolles shot only 53 percent from the foul line and gave up 16 offensive rebounds, the Highlanders (25-6) made them pay.\n\"We tried to box out here and there, but you've got to box out every possession,\" Bolles coach Kelly Stevenson said. \"And they took advantage of that.\"\nValeria Munoz led the onslaught with 20 points, including six of the Highlanders' 11 threes, as Lake Highland Prep found a response from beyond the arc to every Bulldog rally.\nNear the end of the third quarter, the Bulldogs (25-5) trimmed the gap back to single digits on consecutive Evie Freeman baskets late in the third quarter, but Lake Highland Prep slammed the door on a Munoz three.\nLake Highland Prep built a 14-point lead before halftime, pulling down seven offensive rebounds and sinking 5 of 11 shots from 3-point range, advancing to the final at 5:30 p.m. March 11 against top seed Somerset Canyons.\nFreeman scored 11 points and Terrell McCoy added 10 in the final Bolles game for both. Senior starters Leila Goll and Ari Walker also graduate, while Presley Norman, Caelyn Carter, Mamie Burkett andAngelina Laudicina are among Bulldogs on course to return.\n\"I wish it would've gone a different way, but I'm super proud of them,\" Stevenson said. \"The future is bright for Bolles.\"\nThis article originally appeared on Florida Times-Union: Lake Highland Prep's shooting stars deny Bolles girls basketball\n2",
    "summary": "2 days ago Bolles' dreams of a hometown basketball championship finished beneath a 3-point barrage at UNF Arena. When Bolles shot only 53 percent from the foul line and gave up 16 offensive rebounds, the Highlanders (25-6) made them pay. Lake Highland Prep built a 14-point lead before halftime, pulling down seven offensive rebounds and sinking 5 of 11 shots from 3-point range, advancing to the final at 5:30 p.m. March 11 against top seed Somerset Canyons.",
